#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "bc48e845-b2a7-5e30-9c73-b7d842f4090c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5383" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $\\left(6,\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= r\\cos\\theta \\\\[5pt] &= 6 \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= 3 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= r\\sin\\theta \\\\[5pt] &= 6 \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 6 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= 3\\sqrt{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(3, 3\\sqrt{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "3454059c-9847-5bb2-9732-e33885afa462" :question/math-academy-id "e-5384" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $2$, and that $P$ makes an angle of $135^{\\circ}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P$?" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta)$, where\n\n$$\nr=2, \\quad \\theta=135^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= r\\cos\\theta \\\\[5pt] &=2 \\cos\\left(135^{\\circ} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 2 \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -\\sqrt{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= r\\sin\\theta \\\\[5pt] &= 2 \\sin\\left(135^{\\circ}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\sqrt{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\sqrt{2}, \\sqrt{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "63b5b1e6-1885-554c-973c-bd78555f44ec" :question/math-academy-id "e-5385" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $\\left(r, \\dfrac{11\\pi}{6} \\right)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $\\left(5r,\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3,-\\sqrt{3})$." :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r$, the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} r &= \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\\\[3pt] &=\\sqrt{3^2+(\\sqrt{3})^2} \\\\[3pt] &= \\sqrt{12} \\\\[3pt] &= 2\\sqrt{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(5(2\\sqrt{3}),\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)=\\left(10\\sqrt{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= r\\cos\\theta \\\\[5pt] &=10\\sqrt{3} \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 10\\sqrt{3} \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -15 \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= r\\sin\\theta \\\\[5pt] &=10\\sqrt{3} \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 10\\sqrt{3} \\cdot\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\[5pt] &=5\\sqrt{3} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-15, 5\\sqrt{3}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092059 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "f0c5bd75-dd47-5028-8eff-38c87e7411bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-7116" :question/problem "The diagram below shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ and $m \\angle AOB=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/936/Source/Images/c17d33994aa8ae8b77b05cec0df22687.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates\n\n$$\n\\left(3,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe must have $r=3$. Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $3$, the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $3$.\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B$. Since the $\\theta$-coordinate of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthe $\\theta$-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n\\theta_B = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(3,\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x &= r\\cos\\theta \\\\[5pt] &=3\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &=3\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &=-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= r\\sin\\theta \\\\[5pt] &= 3\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= 3\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(-\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2},\\dfrac{3\\sqrt 2}{2}\\right)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6fbc8ef-f30c-5f1e-b016-24b45c931a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265794} {:answer/id #uuid "572c37de-c6e1-5111-ae49-cd7109e007da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265795} {:answer/id #uuid "2c07b934-6c82-5ff9-8e4e-c582f03b2d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265796} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa31c6e-d533-5d17-8e4e-2327ffacc325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265797} {:answer/id #uuid "ea306d4e-4767-5838-a873-081559cb2fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "572c37de-c6e1-5111-ae49-cd7109e007da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265795} :answer-field/id #uuid "f37d886e-a3db-54ed-8136-8135816a8801" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265793}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "193e517a-c8a3-5593-924d-bbd3ab8f46f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120311" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265799 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "597879b4-34e1-5300-acf7-fc9ab54b42ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265801} {:answer/id #uuid "c26a53a0-91c4-52d4-baab-d09ef8fabb4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265802} {:answer/id #uuid "9926c3e4-724d-5bf3-b4f6-ba23068c972c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265803} {:answer/id #uuid "723bc5ed-fa57-5534-be9f-fb42a5237071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} {:answer/id #uuid "438f43d4-5f45-5c1a-b6af-5f42ad3c058b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6\\sqrt{2},6\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "723bc5ed-fa57-5534-be9f-fb42a5237071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265804} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb2da5f-b177-521a-89fc-c3bd4625394a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265800}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd295ef1-565e-5082-b9b4-b1e2a3d77e84" :question/math-academy-id "q-120328" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0599bbf-0a2a-5bf6-acdd-acbe5d243900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265808} {:answer/id #uuid "bc77e445-0135-5876-a0a7-dec8d36ff865" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265809} {:answer/id #uuid "b57ebf74-1109-5e2c-aced-3cda5c4c6308" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265810} {:answer/id #uuid "66c4b605-0aca-51e3-b8fe-e6c77be3a459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265811} {:answer/id #uuid "98ab9bcc-c3cf-50a5-8d34-6919868f579e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c4b605-0aca-51e3-b8fe-e6c77be3a459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265811} :answer-field/id #uuid "2def96d6-5ad6-5ae8-a97f-084f0839a124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265807}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "256dfcfa-6ea4-5478-bf71-0a81ef7db2c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-120468" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0713e35e-6435-5ac5-b404-deb8a7f6b610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265815} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a250a8-3dfa-5f81-8455-561a61b5b21a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265816} {:answer/id #uuid "993e94ea-20f3-5b92-bfb2-0985049bca80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265817} {:answer/id #uuid "2b957cb0-8603-5450-8ba2-baad7c038789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265818} {:answer/id #uuid "777a4b0d-d347-581d-879a-af2dca7cbe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0713e35e-6435-5ac5-b404-deb8a7f6b610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265815} :answer-field/id #uuid "aee33c74-a634-5edd-9349-06ba0d8bbd43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265814}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "de8834e5-df0a-5c36-914b-a1ed971ccd20" :question/math-academy-id "q-120595" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265820 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59089e7d-733a-5a7d-9851-d20e38c5e458" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265822} {:answer/id #uuid "153bf9d5-5ec5-55ff-96e2-6a413e26cff2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265823} {:answer/id #uuid "0e2ab7a2-ece3-55d4-a636-667e4ec5fcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265824} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b14ad3-33d1-50ce-9cd6-5969e82aba55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265825} {:answer/id #uuid "d9ce342e-755b-590d-8890-1181d0837260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2b14ad3-33d1-50ce-9cd6-5969e82aba55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265825} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce045a4f-b2e7-5441-89c8-84712af99447" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265821}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae9fb0df-51c8-5805-8a9a-3fb0f6d09879" :question/math-academy-id "q-120597" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad30021-bc43-57e0-a820-2710f02ca5db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265829} {:answer/id #uuid "709161f6-4301-5a31-9254-6d1dbf756fd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265830} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bf1565-973f-5ec9-af0a-b3a28afc975a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265831} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b67b96-8fc7-5580-b625-6fbc1c3d1c60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265832} {:answer/id #uuid "12faf59b-0877-575e-89b5-871b697d27e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0bf1565-973f-5ec9-af0a-b3a28afc975a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265831} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b9bf650-bc26-5590-ae1f-8c494f3990fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265828}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f581deec-3496-5aa3-b1da-a71e2f13279a" :question/math-academy-id "q-17134" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $4,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=4,\\,\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\ x & =4(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\ y & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da6cc301-2b87-5f1e-897e-d1001f1d936d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265836} {:answer/id #uuid "7c068f4a-dc0a-5730-86d0-3692048e016a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265837} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9bd102-bd8c-5cad-bac3-03bf3c1fdf07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265838} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e0378a-9576-5e7d-a4a5-8dc51f7f479b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265839} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f90cec-568b-5b2d-b613-75c5be456eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8e0378a-9576-5e7d-a4a5-8dc51f7f479b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265839} :answer-field/id #uuid "b75fb267-4801-5d9d-9434-00fe1e1fc10e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265835}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08b1e209-b347-5432-815b-1436ec87c2d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-17137" :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $7,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ where\n\n$$\nr=7,\\,\\theta =0.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ x & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ y & =7\\cdot 0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(7,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab00f58-7043-56bc-80b9-e4b46201457c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab00f58-7043-56bc-80b9-e4b46201457c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c679c82c-67f7-5dc4-8bf8-24e4e95d39aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81de9715-bf88-552b-8e04-4b22a43e1750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81de9715-bf88-552b-8e04-4b22a43e1750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265845} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5954563-d1e1-57ff-ab0d-d5df53561cff" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265844}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7173cc2a-4622-5226-b8ea-06c0c7668125" :question/math-academy-id "q-276511" :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ x & =2\\cdot 0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =2\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "513f9504-104a-5018-8c1d-c98f3d06af66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513f9504-104a-5018-8c1d-c98f3d06af66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265848} :answer-field/id #uuid "62f3ef7f-4ab7-5d1d-ac29-6188319c61aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265847} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba7b8f20-40d3-516b-817b-109a1d5dd0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba7b8f20-40d3-516b-817b-109a1d5dd0c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265850} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a0da30-89e1-5087-a3c4-9e7fa7d7ee6a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answ