swer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc71fdb5-104c-5508-81e0-da213c26e0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc71fdb5-104c-5508-81e0-da213c26e0a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265856} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ec3f6-2f33-5434-8b3b-c33f25b7f9c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43bdda48-0d36-580a-b25b-24055542dda1" :question/math-academy-id "q-276774" :question/problem "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,{300}^{∘}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({300}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b3acb1a-ab0a-5b22-9b41-323026d642fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3acb1a-ab0a-5b22-9b41-323026d642fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265859} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a0f9566-5e77-5620-9dd0-3bfc1a02b3de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265858} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ac96947-efdd-550e-a44e-41f65188f306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ac96947-efdd-550e-a44e-41f65188f306" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265861} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5443125-7f8c-5e98-af18-c55fd5aeeb59" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265860}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67dc0f42-e4f5-5bc1-9f03-440fc8535835" :question/math-academy-id "q-276786" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $8,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=8,\\,\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},-4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87f80908-037b-56ae-a2b5-59bdc49f0acb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given a Description of the Radius and Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057b6b8e-7efa-5e4a-9f17-42680386a2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "057b6b8e-7efa-5e4a-9f17-42680386a2ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265864} :answer-field/id #uuid "9fd9d0c0-f207-55c3-be9d-9b5a92b66369" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c6418e2-73ac-5e70-89f7-cc821a004a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c6418e2-73ac-5e70-89f7-cc821a004a8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265866} :answer-field/id #uuid "f64753f3-c12f-53f3-a5a1-c80e9f76c8c7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265865}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91859d58-dbae-5830-8515-ca5a18c13524" :question/math-academy-id "q-276791" :question/problem "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265867 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcd50afe-3eb7-5386-b4d6-52582e3a30cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcd50afe-3eb7-5386-b4d6-52582e3a30cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265869} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c14cda7-3017-549b-a975-ccfa2bad06de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eebaf68-1aa6-5758-a417-bf5386698813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eebaf68-1aa6-5758-a417-bf5386698813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265871} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9310a9-02c3-566b-aee6-6d2953623dfd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265870}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "188fe0f5-6988-52fa-91db-08219b61d9bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-276803" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c40f843-0631-58b4-a06f-6194eb70da85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c40f843-0631-58b4-a06f-6194eb70da85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265874} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a9ad845-ec06-5c93-a188-15ae28b31b4a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265873} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8753972-949b-559c-ae7f-2d99a21abb54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265876}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8753972-949b-559c-ae7f-2d99a21abb54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265876} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d93c35-a9f4-5a65-afe7-4b3454045b6d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265875}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9180b14-eb26-561a-9fe7-e0f704e71580" :question/math-academy-id "q-276838" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265877 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c59c4311-7e00-5f7f-8733-9fa2f55748aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c59c4311-7e00-5f7f-8733-9fa2f55748aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265879} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f71cd2d-b7fb-5953-aada-7492f4f7add2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265878} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "967cbe30-b750-5581-a87f-808a1ee4c15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "967cbe30-b750-5581-a87f-808a1ee4c15d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265881} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f039044-546d-51dc-a1c5-66b80a481b23" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265880}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a83f5e13-021d-5699-bc54-d8ad3c050b9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-277012" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/d931b377a8d62bf3b56672a0f556c0581e8400c5b1c7b1f783f08e4297342855.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={210}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{210}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf700d8-e674-5c42-bdca-25ab22c82965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caf700d8-e674-5c42-bdca-25ab22c82965" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265884} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6b5ca2-baf7-510e-8c4f-1ee7f6e5f63b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265883} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a403334-4552-5733-b103-9125811367ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a403334-4552-5733-b103-9125811367ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265886} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b14c52f-ef19-5ae5-bb78-f8a0e01ae8dd" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265885}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "01215fdb-7672-5686-a3c5-4762cb00c7f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-277027" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4325b6f4-81a3-52d0-9d82-c6657defe646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265889} {:answer/id #uuid "7da24958-c102-5419-802f-6adc6f1cd558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265890} {:answer/id #uuid "c582e450-fd13-5f62-8885-2162059c2da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},4\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265891} {:answer/id #uuid "1074de6c-fc5b-5bc2-b180-1d1afa758926" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265892} {:answer/id #uuid "20130431-0d2d-531e-b8c8-ee3f7bbcff6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8\\sqrt{3},8\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4325b6f4-81a3-52d0-9d82-c6657defe646" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265889} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a8de227-8472-5c81-ab7b-830ec478a832" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265888}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70ccad16-095b-5f4c-aa4b-ada6d051e6fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-40826" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{2},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(8,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(8,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(8,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(8,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8fd58fe-4896-5fe7-aad7-91faa4f78dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{11}{2},-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265896} {:answer/id #uuid "25792196-76aa-5410-907f-17e0ce4f433b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265897} {:answer/id #uuid "343c0eb9-7476-530e-98c3-341f3b5dddb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-4\\sqrt{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265898} {:answer/id #uuid "9614c3f1-7370-5131-b1ed-819663b373c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,-4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265899} {:answer/id #uuid "96b84ae8-5a62-5979-9651-67e8955deacc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25792196-76aa-5410-907f-17e0ce4f433b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,-5\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265897} :answer-field/id #uuid "04433b50-8f27-51fb-aae8-a5df454ceded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265895}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eeed4d5b-d5f2-546d-acc5-ae37a625911e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81224" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/a615f7a80b81f89fccd8c82bf65d65e3a4a65958ec8a6fbc746ae68749949a87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ and $m∠AOB=\\pi ,$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,\\frac{4\\pi }{3})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =10(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,-5\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd8a5005-c0ae-5ac5-8e59-40bd6284e726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265903} {:answer/id #uuid "bd74836d-bdad-5231-8960-30d3bd87b611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265904} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf88997-6b46-5779-8627-ffb5f21c0dfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265905} {:answer/id #uuid "137986cd-bae7-5166-8185-7c24c15f3bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265906} {:answer/id #uuid "aab39890-41b2-55fe-8e41-de736216ccc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137986cd-bae7-5166-8185-7c24c15f3bbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265906} :answer-field/id #uuid "774a89f7-9ae3-5415-b7d7-7ec8885d5975" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265902}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fb5ea40-ef50-5f62-83b4-93e5913563b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-8519" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(6,{45}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate od $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\ y & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45540c78-9e69-5f00-95fb-ed2690378cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265910} {:answer/id #uuid "6ecf2784-822a-58b8-b2ba-f7e819ffd3c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265911} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a925bb-9cd0-5b16-8b7e-ad218de9f1e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,3\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265912} {:answer/id #uuid "b50a1caa-b927-5265-a694-1bdf2b5ddaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265913} {:answer/id #uuid "24cc3c30-cbee-5b85-bb53-cbe94ef9511f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50a1caa-b927-5265-a694-1bdf2b5ddaa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265913} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4df8eef-e36f-5a3e-ab1b-7f3cc68d497f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265909}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbdb76b1-154e-56f4-8c53-1a3f3061c333" :question/math-academy-id "q-86619" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$  Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},3\\sqrt{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(3\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18+18} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(\\frac{6}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=(3,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$"}]}
